","The overuse of antibiotics in the treatment of parasitic infections contributes to the development and spread of antibiotic-resistant strains in primates, as it does in other organisms. This occurs because antibiotics are designed to target and kill bacteria, not parasites. When antibiotics are used on primates with primate-related parasites, the bacteria in their bodies are more likely to develop resistance to the antibiotics, which can then be passed on to other plants and animals. This can lead to a vicious cycle, as the bacteria become more resistant to antibiotics, making it more difficult to treat infections. Additionally, the overuse of antibiotics can lead to a decrease in the diversity of bacteria in the patient's body, which can make them more susceptible to other types of infections.
