Calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction in which 3 moles of carbon monoxide (CO) gas is burned with excess oxygen gas to form 3 moles of carbon dioxide (CO2) gas. The reaction is carried out at constant pressure and a temperature of 298 K.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to find the difference between the entropy of the products and the reactants. The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:

2 CO (g) + O2 (g) → 2 CO2 (g)

We can use the standard molar entropies (S°) for each substance to calculate the change in entropy. The standard molar entropies for CO, O2, and CO2 are 188.7 J/mol·K, 205.0 J/mol·K, and 213.7 J/mol·K, respectively.

ΔS = ΣnS°(products) - ΣnS°(reactants)

For the given reaction:

ΔS = [2 mol CO2 × 213.7 J/mol·K] - [2 mol CO × 188.7 J/mol·K + 1 mol O2 × 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [427.4 J/K] - [377.4 J/K + 205.0 J/K]

ΔS = [427.4 J/K] - [582.4 J/K]

ΔS = -155.0 J/K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -155.0 J/K.
