"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the two nuclei.  
- **π molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the axis between the two nuclei.  

Mathematically, σ orbitals are formed by the constructive interference of atomic orbitals oriented along the axis, while π orbitals are formed by the constructive interference of atomic orbitals oriented perpendicular to the axis.  

This difference in electron density distribution leads to distinct properties:  
- σ bonds are stronger and more stable than π bonds because the electron density is more concentrated between the nuclei, leading to stronger electrostatic attraction.  
- σ bonds are also more resistant to rotation than π bonds because the electron density is not spread out in the plane perpendicular to the axis.  

Thus, σ bonds are typically stronger and more stable than π bonds.  

\boxed{\text{σ orbital: electron density along the axis, σ bond stronger and rigid}}  
\boxed{\text{π orbital: electron density above/below the axis, π bond weaker and flexible}}
