","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: This occurs when molecules have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms in their structures. In C4H10O, there are two types of structural isomerism:

   a. Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon skeleton of the molecule is different. For example, butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and 2-methylpropan-1-ol (CH3CH(OH)CH3) are chain isomers.

   b. Functional group isomerism: This occurs when the atoms are connected in the same sequence but have different functional groups. For example, C4H10O can be either an alcohol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) or an ether (CH3OCH3).

2. Optical isomerism: This occurs when a molecule has a chiral center, which is a carbon atom bonded to four different groups. If a molecule has a chiral center, it can exist in two non-superimposable mirror-image forms called enantiomers. In C4H10O, the molecule 2-butanol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3) has a chiral center at the second carbon atom and can exhibit optical isomerism.

In summary, the possible types of isomerism in a molecule with the chemical formula C4H10O are:

- Chain isomerism (structural isomerism)
- Functional group isomerism (structural isomerism)
-Optical isomerism (stereoisomerism)
