<start_of_turn>model
","In a double-stranded DNA molecule, the bases pair according to the following rules: adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). Therefore, the number of each base in the other strand will be equal to the number of its complementary base in the first strand.

In this case, the first strand has 20 adenines (A), so the second strand will have 20 thyines (T). Similarly, the first strand has 25 guanines (G), so the second strand will have 25 cytosines (C), and the first strand has 30 cytosines (C), so the second strand will have 30 guanines (G).

To find the total number of each base in the complete double-stranded DNA molecule, we add the numbers from both strands:

A = 20 (first strand) + 20 (second strand) = 40
G = 25 (first strand) + 25 (second strand) = 50
C = 30 (first strand) + 30 (second strand) = 60
T = 22 (first strand) + 20 (second strand) = 42

So, the complete double-stranded DNA molecule contains 40 adenines, 50 guanines, 60 cytosines, and 42 thymines.
