","The electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles is influenced by the pH of the solution in a colloidal system of known ionic strength. The pH affects the surface charge of the colloidal particles, which in turn influences the electrokinetic potential. At low pH values, the surface of the colloidal particles becomes positively charged due to the protonation of functional groups on the surface. As the pH increases, the surface charge becomes more negative as the protons are deprotonated, leading to a higher electrokinetic potential. The relationship between pH and electrokinetic potential can be described by the isoelectric point (IEP) of the colloidal particles. The IEP is the pH at which the colloidal particles have a net surface charge of zero, and the electrokinetic potential is also zero. Below the IEP, the colloidal particles are positively charged, while above the pH of the IEP, they are negatively charged. The isoelectric point is a critical parameter for understanding the stability of colloidal systems, as it determines the degree of electrostatic repulsion between particles and influences their aggregation or flocculation.
