","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis that allows the production of a single enantiomer (a single stereoisomer) of a chiral molecule with 100% enantiomeric excess (ee). This means that the reaction produces only the desired enantiomer, with no contamination from the other enantiomer.

The key difference between absolute asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis is that it provides absolute information about the configuration of the product. In other asymmetric synthesis methods, the reaction may produce a high ee of the desired enantiomer, but the absolute configuration (e.g., R or S) may not be known.

To achieve absolute asymmetric synthesis, chemists typically use chiral auxiliaries or catalysts that have a known absolute configuration. The chiral auxiliary is introduced to the reaction mixture and reacts with the substrate to form a chiral intermediate. The reaction proceeds with high enantioselectivity, and the chiral auxiliary can be removed at the end of the reaction to yield the pure enantiomer of the desired product.

An example of asymmetric synthesis is the synthesis of the chiral amino acid (S)-phenylalanine using the chiral auxiliary method. In this reaction, a chiral auxiliary is added to the substrate, and the reaction proceeds with high enantioselectivity to form the (S)-enantiomer of the amino acid. The chiral auxiliary can then be removed, leaving behind the pure (S)-phenylalanine.

In contrast, in asymmetric asymmetric synthesis, the reaction would not only produce the (S)-enantiomer of the amino acid but would also provide information about the absolute configuration of the chiral center in the product. This information can be determined by using techniques such as X-ray crystallography or polarimatory measurements.

In summary, asymmetric asymmetric synthesis is a more complete and informative approach to asymmetric synthesis, as it not only provides high enantioselectivity but also provides absolute information about the configuration of the product.
