<start_of_turn>model
","The optimal ratio of titanium dioxide (TiO2) nanoparticles and graphene oxide (GO) in a composite material for use in high-performance dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs) is not a fixed value and depends on several factors, such as the specific synthesis method, the size and shape of the nanoparticles, the type of graphene oxide, and the desired properties of the final material. However, in general, a lower ratio of GO to TiO2 is generally preferred for achieving better performance in DSSCs.

The optimal ratio of TiO2 to GO in a composite material for use in high-performance DSSCs is typically in the range of 1:1 to 10:1, with the TiO2 content being the higher of the two. This ratio can be adjusted to achieve the desired balance between the properties of the two materials.

The ratio of TiO2 to GO affects the overall efficiency and stability of the solar cell in the following ways:

1. Light absorption: TiO2 is a wide bandgap semiconductor that can absorb light in the ultraviolet (UV) and visible regions of the spectrum. However, its absorption is limited in the visible range. The addition of GO can improve the light absorption of the composite material by acting as a light-trapping layer and enhancing the light-to-electron conversion efficiency.

