","To prove this, we use the definition of a polyhedral cone and the properties of linear spaces.

1. **Existence of the hyperplane**:
   - The cone \( C \) is defined as the set of all linear combinations of \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \) with nonnegative coefficients.
   - The set \( \{a_1, \ldots, a_n\} \) is a basis of \( C \), so the span of these vectors is \( C \).
   - Since \( b \notin C \), the span of \( \{a_1, \ldots, a_n\} \) and \( b \) is \( \mathbb{R}^n \).
   - The hyperplane \( H \) is the set of all \( x \) such that \( \text{span}(a_1, \ldots, a_n) \cap x = 0 \).
   - The hyperplane \( H \) is nonzero because \( b \notin \text{span}(a_1, \ldots, a_n) \).

2. **Properties of \( H \)**:
   - Since \( H \) is a hyperplane passing through \( 0 \), it divides \( \mathbb{R}^n \) into two half-spaces.
   - Since \( C \) is a cone, all its vectors are nonnegative multiples of \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \), and \( H \) is orthogonal to \( \text{span}(a_1, \ldots, a_n) \), \( C \) lies entirely in one of the half-spaces determined by \( H \).
   - Since \( b \notin C \), \( b \) must lie in the other half-space.

3. **Existence of \( y \)**:
   - The hyperplane \( H \) can be represented by a nonzero linear function \( y \in (\mathbb{R}^n)^* \) such that \( y x = 0 \) for all \( x \) on \( H \).
   - Since \( y \) is nonzero, \( y \neq 0 \).
   - Since \( y \) is a linear function, \( y a_i \geq 0 \) for all \( i = 1, \ldots, n \) because \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \) are all in \( C \).
   - Since \( b \) lies on the other half-space, \( y b < 0 \).

Thus, we have shown that for any point \( b \notin C \), there exists a nonzero linear form \( y \in (\mathbb{R}^n)^* \) such that \( y a_i \geq 0 \) for all \( i = 1, \ldots, n \), and \( y b < 0 \).
