","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in QuickSearch in the best-case scenario is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case**: If the input list \( n \) has only one element, there are no comparisons required, so \( C(1) = 0 \).
2. **Main Case**: For \( n > 1 \), the base-case case is split into two sub-cases:
   - The pivot is chosen, and it divides the input into two sub-cases of size \( \lfloor n/2 \rfloor \) and \( \lceil n/2 \rceil \).
   - The number of comparisons in the first sub-case is \( C(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor) \).
   - The number of comparisons in the second sub-case is \( C(\lceil n/2 \rceil) \).
   - The number of comparisons for the pivot is \( n - 1 \), as the pivot is compared to all \( n - 1 \) other elements.
   - Thus, the total number of comparisons is \( C(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor) + C(\lceil n/2 \rceil) + n - 1 \).

The reasoning is based on the fact that:
- The number of comparisons is \( n - 1 \) for the pivot.
- The number of comparisons for the two sub-cases is the sum of the number of comparisons in each sub-case.
- The sub-cases are split into equal-length sub-cases, so the number of comparisons in each sub-case is \( C(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor) \) and \( C(\lceil n/2 \rceil) \).

Thus, the recurrence relation is derived by summing the contributions of the pivot and the two sub-cases.
