","No, the statement is not true. The equality \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\) does not hold if and only if \(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\). 

**Explanation**:
1. **\(L_{\alpha}\)** is the \(\alpha\)-th ordinal in the **L-series** (the ""light-set"" series), which is a sequence of sets built from the empty set by the method of ""stepwise extension"" (e.g., \(L_0 = \text{null set}\), \(L_1 = \text{set of all singleton sets}\), \(L_2 = \text{set of all sets of singletons}\), etc).
2. **\(V_{\alpha}\)** is the \(\alpha\)-th ordinal in the **V-series** (the ""universe set"" series), which is the set of all integers, then all pairs of integers, then all sets of pairs of integers, and so on.
3. **\(\beta_{\alpha}\)** is the \(\alpha\)-th **Berchowski number**, defined as the smallest integer \(\beta\) such that \(\beta \geq \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\).

**Key Observations**:
- The \(\beta_{\alpha}\) is defined as the smallest integer of size \(\geq \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\), so \(\left|V_{\alpha}\right| \leq \beta_{\alpha}\).
- The L-series is a sequence of strictly growing powers of powers of \(\omega\), while the V-series is a sequence of powers of powers of \(\omega\) with additional integers.
- For \(\alpha = \omega\), \(\left|L_{\omega}\right| = \left|V_{\omega}\right|\) holds, but \(\omega \neq \beta_{\omega}\).
- For \(\alpha > \omega\), the V-series grows faster than the L-series, so \(\left|V_{\alpha}\right| > \left|L_{\alpha}\right|\) for most \(\alpha\).

**Conclusion**:
The equality \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\) does not hold if and only if \(\alpha = \beta_{\alpha}\). 

\boxed{\text{The statement is not true.}}
