## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
What is the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2\pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\Delta \lambda = \) change in wavelength,
- \(\hbar = \) reduced Planck constant (\(1.0545 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),
- \(m_e = \) electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),
- \(c = \) speed of light (\(3.00 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),
- \(\theta = \) scattering angle.

The maximum wavelength change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (back-scattering), so \(\cos \theta = -1\):

\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2\pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 + 1) = \frac{4\pi \hbar}{m_e c}
\]

The percentage change is:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100
\]

Given \(\lambda_0 = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}\), we substitute the values:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{4\pi \times 1.0545 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 3.00 \times 10^8} \right) \times \frac{1}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100
\]

\[
\text{Percentage change} \approx 0.00035 \, \text{or} \, 0.035\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{0.035\%}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

