</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the activation energy \( E_a \) for the reaction \( H_2 + I_2 \rightarrow 2HI \), we use the Arrhenius equation:

\[
k = A e^{-E_a / RT}
\]

Taking the natural logarithm of both sides:

\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]

Rearranging to solve for \( E_a \):

\[
E_a = -\frac{RT \ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_1} \right)}{\frac{1}{T_2} - \frac{1}{T_1}}
\]

Substitute the given values:

\[
E_a = -\frac{2 \times 10^{-3} \times 2.303 \times \ln \left( \frac{10^4}{10^{-1}} \right)}{\frac{0.0012}{273.15} - \frac{0.0018}{273.15}}
\]

\[
E_a = -\frac{2 \times 10^{-3} \times 2.303 \times \ln (10^5)}{0.0012 - 0.0018}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{2 \times 10^{-3} \times 2.303 \times 10.313}{0.0018 - 0.0012}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{4.70 \times 10^{-2}}{0.0006}
\]

\[
E_a = 78.33 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus, the activation energy for the reaction is \(\boxed{78.33 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}}\).

