The composition of the star also plays a role in determining the outcome. A star with a higher proportion of heavier elements (e.g., iron) will have a lower core temperature and will be more likely to collapse into a neutron star or a black hole. This is because heavier elements are more tightly bound together, and it takes more energy to overcome their repulsion and allow the star to collapse further.

