What is the expression for the intensity of thermal radiation of a black body in terms of the temperature \( T \) and wavelength \( \lambda \)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The intensity of thermal radiation of a black body is given by Planck's law, which is given by:
\[
I(\lambda, T) = \frac{2\pi h c^2}{\lambda^5} \frac{1}{e^{h c / \lambda k T} - 1}
\]
where:
- \( I(\lambda, T) \) is the intensity of radiation at wavelength \( \lambda \) and temperature \( T \).
- \( h \) is Planck's constant (\( 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s} \)),
- \( c \) is the speed of light (\( 2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m s}^{-1} \)),
- \( k \) is the Boltzmann constant (\( 1.381 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J K}^{-1} \)),
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation,
- \( T \) is the temperature of the black body in Kelvin.

This formula shows that the intensity of radiation is proportional to:
- \( \frac{1}{\lambda^5} \): This means that the intensity is inversely proportional to the fifth power of the wavelength, meaning shorter wavelengths (higher frequencies) have higher intensities.
