1. **Calculate the uncertainty in energy (\(\Delta E\))**:
   \[
   \Delta E \times \Delta t \geq \frac{h}{2}
   \]
   \[
   \Delta E \geq \frac{h}{2 \Delta t} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ Js}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 1.66 \times 10^{-11} \text{ J}
   \]
   Convert this to MeV:
   \[
   \Delta E = 1.66 \times 10^{-11} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} = 10.38 \text{ MeV}
   \]

2. **Determine the range of outcomes**:
   The uncertainty in the measured rest energy is \(\Delta E = 10.38 \text{ MeV}\). Therefore, the typical range of outcomes is:
   \[
   1385 \text{ MeV} - 10.38 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1385 \text{ MeV} + 10.38 \text{ MeV}
   \]
   \[
   1374.62 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1395.38 \text{ MeV}
   \]

Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is \(\boxed{1374.62 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1395.38 \text{ MeV}}\).
